    <text><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Clinical status</th><th>Verification</th><th>Code</th><th>Provenance</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr ID="problem-1">
    <td>Progressive hearing loss in both ears over about three years, worse on the left, with poor understanding of soft speech; type and cause not yet established at intake; audiology and otolaryngology evaluation requested</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>H91.93</td><td>S — Synthetic presenting concern anchored to his documented account; superseded by the diagnosis at the audiologic evaluation.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-2">
    <td>Persistent, bothersome tinnitus in both ears for about three years, constant ringing and buzzing day and night, with intolerance of loud sound; synthetic modern episode linked to his documented tinnitus</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>H93.13</td><td>S — Synthetic modern coding of the documented tinnitus.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-3">
    <td>Adjustment disorder with depressed mood in response to progressive hearing loss, with withdrawal from social gatherings; synthetic modern episode linked to his documented despair and isolation</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>F43.21</td><td>S — Synthetic modern diagnosis; the documented letters describe distress and withdrawal, and the record makes no claim about his real mental health.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-4">
    <td>Bilateral sensorineural hearing loss, asymmetric (left worse than right), sloping from normal or mild in the low frequencies to moderately severe in the high frequencies; no air-bone gap; synthetic modern episode linked to his documented progressive deafness, whose cause remains unknown</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>H90.3</td><td>S — Synthetic modern diagnosis made at the audiologic evaluation; it states no cause, because the retrospective theories are disputed.</td>
  </tr></tbody></table></text>

